artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider an observational study designed to evaluate a causal hypothesis by means of an *elaborate theory*: a prespecified, finite collection of distinct predictions $P_1, P_2, \dots, P_k$ about features of the observed data that should hold if the treatment has a genuine causal effect. Each prediction $P_i$ corresponds to a null hypothesis $H_i$ stating that the corresponding feature is absent (i.e., the prediction fails), and is testable by a statistical test $T_i$ producing a p-value $p_i$ on the observed data.

Investigators specify, in advance:
(a) a partial priority ordering on the predictions, reflecting that some predictions are considered more central to the theory than others;
(b) a desire for inferential statements about each individual prediction $P_i$;
(c) a desire for an inferential statement about the overall *fraction of the theory corroborated*, i.e., a confidence statement about the (unknown) number or proportion of the $H_i$ that are false.

Develop a statistical inference procedure that, on the basis of $(p_1,\dots,p_k)$ (or the underlying joint test statistics, allowing arbitrary dependence), simultaneously delivers:

1. A prioritized testing scheme that respects the prespecified priority ordering and controls the familywise error rate at a chosen level $\alpha$ for the global null $\bigcap_i H_i$ and for the ordered subfamilies;
2. Valid individual inferences for each prediction $P_i$ (e.g., adjusted p-values or simultaneous confidence statements for each $H_i$);
3. A simultaneous lower confidence bound, at level $1-\alpha$, on the number (or fraction) of false hypotheses among $\{H_1,\dots,H_k\}$, interpreted as the portion of the elaborate theory corroborated by the data.

The problem is to construct such a procedure with rigorously established type-I error guarantees for all three components jointly, to characterize its power properties, and to determine optimal trade-offs between prioritized testing and inference about the corroborated fraction.

